        "task": "Apply same-direction componentwise addition to all four numeric pairs, preserving the signs of the operands, decimal place value, and strict comparison direction. An option asserts the exact result of this operation, not merely a numerically true inequality.",
        "source_solution": "Independent full solution. (a) 12+9=21 and (-5)+7=2, so 21>2. (b) (-28)+(-10)=-38 and 0+(-6)=-6, so -38<-6. (c) (-2.5)+(-6.5)=-9 and (-0.7)+(-1.3)=-2, so -9<-2; these decimals are exact tenths. (d) 11+0=11 and 7.4+(-8)=-0.6, so 11>-0.6. All eight original inequalities are true and every pair has a common strict direction. The addition theorem applies without positivity assumptions, including negative and zero operands. Reversing both comparisons first is another valid way to handle the > pairs. No convention about a variable domain or endpoints is required for these specified numbers.",
        "unit_reason": "One unit judges the exact componentwise-addition operation across integer, negative, decimal, and zero operands. The four printed subparts are not four separate conceptual questions; selecting all accurate operation records retains all four calculations without a combination code or a result table.",
